00:15:39.787 --> 00:15:43.860
Optional, yes,
that doesn't mean you have to use it.

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:48.287
But when we're asking you in your
employment or work history section,

00:15:48.287 --> 00:15:53.204
information about those roles Sometimes I
see people leave those blank and

00:15:53.204 --> 00:15:56.239
then the application
itself looks very sparse.

00:15:56.239 --> 00:16:00.756
And there's not enough meet in
the resume to help cover that.

00:16:00.756 --> 00:16:03.754
So, as you're thinking about both,

00:16:03.754 --> 00:16:08.064
like every element as a different
angle on who you are,

00:16:08.064 --> 00:16:12.767
I think that's one of those not complete,
like red flags.

00:16:12.767 --> 00:16:17.602
But something for you to think about
as you're doing this like just that

00:16:17.602 --> 00:16:20.028
completeness and effort element.

00:16:20.028 --> 00:16:25.221
&gt;&gt; I'm glad you brought that up,
we asked folks to submit a resume but

00:16:25.221 --> 00:16:30.898
there's also the employment section and
we ask people to fill that out.

00:16:30.898 --> 00:16:36.224
And it is if you see what I would
say is a thinner application

00:16:36.224 --> 00:16:40.465
where some of those places
are not filled out,

00:16:40.465 --> 00:16:46.248
it does beg the question of
the amount of effort you put into it.

00:16:46.248 --> 00:16:50.717
Because certainly while we can see
what you've done on your resume,

00:16:50.717 --> 00:16:54.107
we provide those other places
on the application for

00:16:54.107 --> 00:16:57.205
you to really articulate
things more clearly.

00:16:57.205 --> 00:17:01.916
And it's helpful to us we don't
ask it just to fill the space,

00:17:01.916 --> 00:17:05.632
we genuinely have a desire and
understanding and

00:17:05.632 --> 00:17:09.996
so, be sure that you are putting
your best foot forward.

00:17:09.996 --> 00:17:15.094
And not giving us any instance
where we have to think,

00:17:15.094 --> 00:17:21.227
what kind of effort or
commitment did they have to this process?

00:17:21.227 --> 00:17:24.843
&gt;&gt; Yeah, outside of the optional questions
because those are optional like truly-

00:17:24.843 --> 00:17:26.650
&gt;&gt; Yes, of course, right.

00:17:26.650 --> 00:17:30.518
&gt;&gt; We've been meeting people who have
filled all of them out with admitted

00:17:30.518 --> 00:17:34.007
people who have left them all blank so
know that truly optional.

00:17:34.007 --> 00:17:36.715
&gt;&gt; And I think that's one of
the questions that comes up at probably I

00:17:36.715 --> 00:17:39.434
thought that might be the top line
question would have to answer.

00:17:39.434 --> 00:17:44.359
So yes let me first please reiterate that,
if we say it is optional it is

00:17:44.359 --> 00:17:49.369
genuinely optional and we only want
you to include something if you feel

00:17:49.369 --> 00:17:54.562
like it's compelling, that it provides
you with another opportunity.

00:17:54.562 --> 00:17:58.636
To share something that you may not have
been able to communicate elsewhere in

00:17:58.636 --> 00:17:59.646
the application.

00:17:59.646 --> 00:18:03.968
But we absolutely do not look or
put anyone at a disadvantage.

00:18:03.968 --> 00:18:05.414
If, for instance,

00:18:05.414 --> 00:18:10.784
I do not fill out the optional impact
questions where those short answers.

00:18:10.784 --> 00:18:15.018
&gt;&gt; So, I could not have planted
a better [LAUGH] segue for

00:18:15.018 --> 00:18:17.337
the next top question here.

00:18:17.337 --> 00:18:19.767
We're talking a lot about
connecting the dots.

00:18:19.767 --> 00:18:22.180
Here is some optional spaces.

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:25.950
So and I think it works
perfectly with today's topic.

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:28.239
So, this is a question from Huggy Rao.

00:18:28.239 --> 00:18:31.336
How do you see successful
applicants use the optional essays?

00:18:31.336 --> 00:18:35.530
Do they answer all of them present to
provide as many pieces as possible or

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:38.915
answer fewer to better highlight
the important aspects?

00:18:38.915 --> 00:18:45.090
When might be a time to use
those impact questions?

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:47.808
Let's shed some light there.

00:18:47.808 --> 00:18:51.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there's so
many ways that they can be used and

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:56.487
I've seen it done in many ways and
there's no one right way to do it.

00:18:56.487 --> 00:19:00.738
Oftentimes the impact prompts
can be a great opportunity for

00:19:00.738 --> 00:19:04.404
you to elaborate on
a particular extracurricular or

00:19:04.404 --> 00:19:08.320
activity or community organization
you are a part of and

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:11.924
to talk a little bit about
maybe the impact you had.

00:19:11.924 --> 00:19:16.275
And again, I want people to
understand that the impact

00:19:16.275 --> 00:19:19.214
doesn't have to be at a large scale.

00:19:19.214 --> 00:19:24.713
It can also be the impact you've had
with a mentee or as you were tutoring or

00:19:24.713 --> 00:19:31.103
coaching one individual and help support
that person as they were navigating there,

00:19:31.103 --> 00:19:35.560
either their educational journey or
even career journey.

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:40.945
So, I see the impact essays as
a nice way to elaborate on some

00:19:40.945 --> 00:19:46.440
of the things you have done and
maybe have not glossed over but

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:52.283
we're able to elaborate as much
on our part in the application.

00:19:52.283 --> 00:19:57.539
It's also nice to see there might be
something on your resume that you've

00:19:57.539 --> 00:20:02.456
indicated that you're really proud
of because it was a big success or

00:20:02.456 --> 00:20:03.833
accomplishment.

00:20:03.833 --> 00:20:07.543
And then to be able to take one piece or
component of that and

00:20:07.543 --> 00:20:09.986
really give us a little bit more meat.

00:20:09.986 --> 00:20:13.407
So, it's all about getting evidence.

00:20:13.407 --> 00:20:17.450
So, we are evidence based in
terms of how we evaluate.

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:19.304
We want to see what you've done.

00:20:19.304 --> 00:20:24.198
And I do believe that the impact
prompts give us that opportunity

00:20:24.198 --> 00:20:26.472
to step a little bit smaller.

00:20:26.472 --> 00:20:28.281
They're like snippets,

00:20:28.281 --> 00:20:33.706
it's not something that you necessarily
need to incorporate into an essay but

00:20:33.706 --> 00:20:38.323
they can be small vignettes of
ways in which, and most impactful.

00:20:38.323 --> 00:20:41.303
&gt;&gt; I'm about to start
using small vignettes.

00:20:41.303 --> 00:20:42.099
&gt;&gt; I just give up.

00:20:42.099 --> 00:20:43.016
&gt;&gt; I love that.

00:20:43.016 --> 00:20:46.677
Yeah because like you're
saying your resume says what?

00:20:46.677 --> 00:20:49.544
Right, oftentimes that's how
the resume is structured.

00:20:49.544 --> 00:20:50.971
Like, this is what I did.

00:20:50.971 --> 00:20:52.271
This is what I did.

00:20:52.271 --> 00:20:56.609
But that often misses we don't have
enough space to talk about the how.

00:20:56.609 --> 00:20:58.519
&gt;&gt; Right?
&gt;&gt; If you're thinking about some of

00:20:58.519 --> 00:21:00.105
those leadership competencies..

00:21:00.105 --> 00:21:01.871
How did you get that thing to happen?

00:21:01.871 --> 00:21:03.527
What was the ripple effect?

00:21:03.527 --> 00:21:09.597
Or why was it significant for
that person or organization or place?

00:21:09.597 --> 00:21:13.958
And sometimes that next step
of helping us understand,

00:21:13.958 --> 00:21:16.751
what did your footprint look like?

00:21:16.751 --> 00:21:19.412
You don't necessarily have
that same space in a resume.

00:21:19.412 --> 00:21:22.881
I would also say sometimes in the essays,
right,

00:21:22.881 --> 00:21:25.617
sometimes you may refer to something.

00:21:25.617 --> 00:21:26.147
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:21:26.147 --> 00:21:31.527
But Stephanie, do you remember
work-shopping the wording for

00:21:31.527 --> 00:21:33.922
these optional questions?

00:21:33.922 --> 00:21:35.011
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yes
&gt;&gt; Like,

00:21:35.011 --> 00:21:37.488
how precise we were trying to
be to make sure it was clear.

00:21:37.488 --> 00:21:42.466
I think for the folks out there, even
letting you know what the historical like

00:21:42.466 --> 00:21:47.230
how these questions were born might be
helpful and how to understand them.

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:51.459
So in the past, so
we've had the what matters most to you and

00:21:51.459 --> 00:21:53.834
why essay for a really long time.

00:21:53.834 --> 00:21:59.103
And overtime, we're finding that people
were trying to put accomplishments.

00:21:59.103 --> 00:22:01.360
&gt;&gt; All accomplishments they
were stuffing accomplishments.

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:01.938
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and

00:22:01.938 --> 00:22:05.992
it was definitely being proud of and
we definitely want to learn about them.

00:22:05.992 --> 00:22:10.580
But it didn't always fit with the flow of
what they were talking about as mattering

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:14.849
or it made essays seem like here's what
matters and here's evidence versus

00:22:14.849 --> 00:22:15.367
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:22:15.367 --> 00:22:16.457
&gt;&gt; The why?

00:22:16.457 --> 00:22:18.492
&gt;&gt; Let me explain why.

00:22:18.492 --> 00:22:22.063
And while it is important for
you to explain why something matters,

00:22:22.063 --> 00:22:25.165
it doesn't need to be like I did X,
Y and Z and that's why.

00:22:25.165 --> 00:22:26.146
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:22:26.146 --> 00:22:30.522
that's where the, the genesis of these
optional questions came into play.

00:22:30.522 --> 00:22:35.358
This allows you to both answer kind of
to the fullest and however you want in

00:22:35.358 --> 00:22:40.350
any of those other spaces, and then
still have dedicated space to allow that

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:45.284
give us those kind of how, why,
what of the accomplishments you've had.

00:22:45.284 --> 00:22:49.179
And that's why there's three as well, like
they don't always have to be connected.

00:22:49.179 --> 00:22:52.133
Sometimes accomplishments come in
many different parts of your life.

00:22:52.133 --> 00:22:56.675
There doesn't even have to be a references
of anywhere else in the application.

00:22:56.675 --> 00:22:59.243
Each of those can be standalone, or

00:22:59.243 --> 00:23:03.446
they can be used to kind of
give more depth somewhere else.

00:23:03.446 --> 00:23:04.206
So-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:04.206 --> 00:23:07.748
&gt;&gt; That is a very long winded way of
saying use it in a way that feels right

00:23:07.748 --> 00:23:08.280
for you.

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:09.363
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and

00:23:09.363 --> 00:23:13.323
I like I think it is important
to reiterate that what we

00:23:13.323 --> 00:23:17.459
genuinely are looking for and
know what matters most and

00:23:17.459 --> 00:23:23.009
why is to find out who you are as a person
and what is most meaningful for you.

00:23:23.009 --> 00:23:27.869
And we want you to go deep in a way that
allows you to share those reasons and

00:23:27.869 --> 00:23:32.811
we want you to take maybe some of this
other stuff, which you may have put in

00:23:32.811 --> 00:23:37.435
there because you felt like you
still wanted to share those stories,

00:23:37.435 --> 00:23:39.051
which we value as well.

00:23:39.051 --> 00:23:42.691
But now you have another
space to put those and so

00:23:42.691 --> 00:23:47.696
that's the intent the impact essay or
the impact short answers,

00:23:47.696 --> 00:23:52.650
and I would say that as well
shared like they don't have to be.

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:55.862
Be intertwined,
they can be three disparate and

00:23:55.862 --> 00:23:58.770
that's probably typical I would even say.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:04.971
Don't try to fill the space if you don't
genuinely have something significant

00:24:04.971 --> 00:24:08.489
to share you may have already
conveyed what you needed to.

00:24:08.489 --> 00:24:13.349
You can do to strong, you could have
one strong one that really speaks to

00:24:13.349 --> 00:24:16.030
a way in which you've been impactful.

00:24:16.030 --> 00:24:21.220
So we don't want you to water down but
also trying to just give ourselves three.

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:26.117
If you feel that you can share impact more

00:24:26.117 --> 00:24:30.871
succinctly in one or two, so
&gt;&gt; I'm loving this

00:24:30.871 --> 00:24:34.551
I'm loving these questions in the Q&amp;A box,
so

00:24:34.551 --> 00:24:37.725
we're already like,
&gt;&gt; Deviating from our script, [LAUGH]

00:24:37.725 --> 00:24:40.389
&gt;&gt; This is way more way more fun.

00:24:40.389 --> 00:24:44.630
So someone is asking about how do
we convey intellectual vitality and

00:24:44.630 --> 00:24:46.761
leadership in what matters most?

00:24:46.761 --> 00:24:48.544
There's going to be some
myth busting here and

00:24:48.544 --> 00:24:50.860
I think that's why this
one made me light up.

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:54.964
Alumns have said this essay must
be from somewhere heartfelt and

00:24:54.964 --> 00:25:00.120
vulnerable, I wonder how to talk
about intellect and leadership here.

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:04.026
I think this is such a fascinating
question because you're understanding

00:25:04.026 --> 00:25:07.610
both hey, what are the things
that the GSB is looking for?

00:25:07.610 --> 00:25:11.090
How can I be thoughtful
about what I'm writing?

00:25:11.090 --> 00:25:13.983
And like, how do I balance
like all of the advice and

00:25:13.983 --> 00:25:18.990
different things that I'm hearing and
of course, that myth busting element.

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:22.671
So Stephany, let's talk a little
bit about the essays right,

00:25:22.671 --> 00:25:26.400
how do they fit in with the greater
picture of application?

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:32.246
What makes a quote unquote well, let's say
helpful essay as opposed to a good or bad.

00:25:32.246 --> 00:25:36.342
because even shifting our mind frame about
how we use the essays might help people

00:25:36.342 --> 00:25:39.600
feel more comfortable with
what they're writing.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:45.190
And then let's unpack this,
like the necessity of being heartfelt and

00:25:45.190 --> 00:25:51.085
vulnerable, like that has a lot,
&gt;&gt; That's heavy yeah, that's deep.

00:25:51.085 --> 00:25:57.210
We want you to be introspective,
we want to hear your authentic voice.

00:25:57.210 --> 00:26:00.984
So those things are important,

00:26:00.984 --> 00:26:05.316
the best way to describe the essay and

00:26:05.316 --> 00:26:10.926
how it can most effectively
convey who you are.

00:26:10.926 --> 00:26:16.146
Is to have someone who knows you really,
really well read your essay and

00:26:16.146 --> 00:26:19.985
make sure it sounds like you and
authentically you.

00:26:19.985 --> 00:26:24.557
Not that necessarily has to be vulnerable,
you do not have to,

00:26:24.557 --> 00:26:30.526
we do those talk sessions, and if there's
anything I've learned from the talk.

00:26:30.526 --> 00:26:35.094
And for those of you on
the line if you're unaware,

00:26:35.094 --> 00:26:41.460
this is a really sort of a GSB
tradition of students sharing a story.

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:44.312
With their classmates that might
also be very heartfelt and

00:26:44.312 --> 00:26:48.470
vulnerable that's where you're starting
to see those elements come in.

00:26:48.470 --> 00:26:53.627
And I have heard them say
in the coaching of that

00:26:53.627 --> 00:26:59.324
you can share as much as
you're comfortable with.

00:26:59.324 --> 00:27:04.078
Without going to a level
that puts you in a position

00:27:04.078 --> 00:27:08.180
that really can be difficult for you.

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:12.361
Right, so it's about understanding
the learning part or

00:27:12.361 --> 00:27:16.210
stepping up from what you
learn from the experience.

00:27:16.210 --> 00:27:20.467
So I guess as I see this,
I want you to be authentic,

00:27:20.467 --> 00:27:23.339
I want to see your authentic voice.

00:27:23.339 --> 00:27:29.226
And be genuine about it, who it is and
it should speak to you but

00:27:29.226 --> 00:27:33.660
you don't have to feel like you have to.

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:37.599
Your heart has to bleed to
get something on the paper

00:27:37.599 --> 00:27:41.183
that's going to be as you said,
helpful for us.

00:27:41.183 --> 00:27:42.495
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:27:42.495 --> 00:27:47.441
&gt;&gt; I don't know how to share that, that
it's it might be a really funny thing,

00:27:47.441 --> 00:27:51.634
might be something that's like
just really fun and important.

00:27:51.634 --> 00:27:57.677
But it is who you are as a person, right
and so that I feel like needs to be part.

00:27:57.677 --> 00:28:02.877
It does not have to be this like I
found myself in the depths of so

00:28:02.877 --> 00:28:08.880
[LAUGH] I don't know how to describe but
it's how do you want to say that?

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:11.520
Well, I know you've seen
some of these essays too,

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:14.680
I don't know they come in all
different shapes and colors.

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:18.850
&gt;&gt; Exactly that heartfelt piece,
like you said, that's you, right?

00:28:18.850 --> 00:28:24.198
Talking about the things that
are important to you, that piece and

00:28:24.198 --> 00:28:28.989
then the vulnerable element,
I define that very broadly.

00:28:28.989 --> 00:28:32.341
Being vulnerable means sharing can
be mean sharing something that's

00:28:32.341 --> 00:28:34.010
meaningful for you.

00:28:34.010 --> 00:28:36.270
Sometimes it's the things
that you care the most about.

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:41.205
That's when you feel the most vulnerable
when you're putting it out there because

00:28:41.205 --> 00:28:44.570
you don't know how the world may or
may not kind of react to it.

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:49.289
And so I would say don't
always think vulnerable as

00:28:49.289 --> 00:28:53.568
a place of fear or
something you're hiding but

00:28:53.568 --> 00:28:59.350
vulnerable in sharing something
that's important to you.

00:28:59.350 --> 00:29:03.358
And that can be a realization
from something more personal or

00:29:03.358 --> 00:29:07.220
it can be something that you
talk about everyday, right.

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:09.750
It's that feeling that
you have connected to it.

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:12.270
And then in terms of
the intellectual vitality and

00:29:12.270 --> 00:29:16.020
leadership I don't think you need
to strategize too much about it.

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:20.189
That question in particular is more
about that self reflection element,

00:29:20.189 --> 00:29:22.820
De Moth talks a lot about
the body of research.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:25.760
That say some of the most successful
leaders are the ones who lead

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:27.401
from a place of mission and values.

00:29:27.401 --> 00:29:30.985
And this is a way for
you to start discovering those and

00:29:30.985 --> 00:29:36.099
letting us know what are some of yours and
what are some of your motivations?

00:29:36.099 --> 00:29:41.545
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a very good point,
I do think it is something I used to

00:29:41.545 --> 00:29:47.405
share when I would be on the road that,
[COUGH] If you do your essay well.

00:29:47.405 --> 00:29:52.519
Or if you really spend the time to focus
on it, it is likely something that's so

00:29:52.519 --> 00:29:55.630
core to the person that
you are to you as a being.

00:29:55.630 --> 00:29:59.857
That it's a theme that resonates
throughout your life and

00:29:59.857 --> 00:30:04.180
this individual mentioned that
they had heard from alums.

00:30:04.180 --> 00:30:09.237
And what I do hear from alums and
students is that if done well,

00:30:09.237 --> 00:30:14.319
it's the kind of essay that if
you read it 10 years post GSB.

00:30:14.319 --> 00:30:19.092
The common denominator that
theme still resonates for

00:30:19.092 --> 00:30:22.002
the person that you are, right?

00:30:22.002 --> 00:30:25.920
And it's the way that you make decisions
whether you know it or not, right?

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:31.891
So when faced with a decision where
you can go in two directions,

00:30:31.891 --> 00:30:37.550
your core is what makes you decide
to go one way or the other.

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:42.519
And those are the kind of things that
we hope to kind of hear about whatever

00:30:42.519 --> 00:30:44.380
that might be for you.

00:30:44.380 --> 00:30:48.317
But the thing that follows
you throughout your life and

00:30:48.317 --> 00:30:51.319
is the essence of the person that you are.

00:30:51.319 --> 00:30:55.015
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I've heard of alums who,
when faced with life choices,

00:30:55.015 --> 00:30:58.856
maybe it's career maybe like,
they've gone back to that essay.

00:30:58.856 --> 00:30:59.580
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:01.358
&gt;&gt; What was driving me was the resume,

00:31:01.358 --> 00:31:03.793
it's like how am I going to
use that going forward?

00:31:03.793 --> 00:31:04.720
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:08.837
&gt;&gt; I'm not going to lie [LAUGH] I use it
as I was writing cover letters to be like,

00:31:08.837 --> 00:31:11.719
this is why I'm making this decision, so,
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:11.719 --> 00:31:16.494
&gt;&gt; Even beyond the application [LAUGH]
what you learned from this question,

00:31:16.494 --> 00:31:17.136
&gt;&gt; Right,

00:31:17.136 --> 00:31:22.458
it's like an almost every decision,
for me as I shared previously,

00:31:22.458 --> 00:31:27.780
this value of curiosity and
wanting to learn and explore like.

00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:33.229
When faced with a decision, I'm always
going to try to go with the like let's

00:31:33.229 --> 00:31:38.260
go out and see that or travel and
understand and just be more curious.

00:31:38.260 --> 00:31:40.323
So that too is all about, yeah.

00:31:41.443 --> 00:31:45.159
&gt;&gt; Yeah and a couple more questions while
we're still on this topic of essays and

00:31:45.159 --> 00:31:49.953
then we will get around to a couple others
from different parts of the application.

00:31:49.953 --> 00:31:53.932
There's a question here from
Chieffing about like How do you feel

00:31:53.932 --> 00:31:57.953
when you read a great,
what matters most essay?

00:31:57.953 --> 00:32:00.582
What are some of the things that
make those standout to you?

00:32:04.498 --> 00:32:09.428
&gt;&gt; Well, I think it is when I feel
like I'm at the end of the essay

00:32:09.428 --> 00:32:14.069
that I can say to myself, okay,
I understand a little bit

00:32:14.069 --> 00:32:18.640
more who this person is now,
this is your application.

00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:22.782
This by no means demonstrates
the person you are, [LAUGH] there's so

00:32:22.782 --> 00:32:26.520
much more to you than what you
can put in the application.

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:33.620
But it gives us a piece and a little bit
of a window into who you are as a person.

00:32:33.620 --> 00:32:38.777
So that's a very vague way to answer but
for me that's,

00:32:38.777 --> 00:32:43.728
if I come out of that knowing,
okay, now I get.

00:32:43.728 --> 00:32:47.460
because I think the rest of
the application really does give you

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:52.840
an opportunity to describe what you've
done and the impact that you've had.

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:57.582
Things that definitely you've
had some control of and

00:32:57.582 --> 00:33:02.745
then this allows you to really
elaborate on you as a person.

00:33:02.745 --> 00:33:06.538
So,
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that's how,

00:33:06.538 --> 00:33:12.000
even if you're writing the same
thing as a ton of other people,

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:17.256
when it's your interpretation of it and
its effect on you and

00:33:17.256 --> 00:33:21.299
who you are,
that immediately sets it apart.

00:33:21.299 --> 00:33:25.935
I don't think there's any topic
that is used too commonly because

00:33:25.935 --> 00:33:30.750
the way that people interpret it
is always going to be different.

00:33:30.750 --> 00:33:36.182
So celebrate the one that you kind
of are feeling, embrace that one.

00:33:36.182 --> 00:33:40.943
And I think sometimes the hardest thing to
tell people is, giving them the power to

00:33:40.943 --> 00:33:44.814
do because they're like,
now I can go in so many directions.

00:33:44.814 --> 00:33:48.107
And as you're reading,
you're going to be having to

00:33:48.107 --> 00:33:53.430
choose [LAUGH] through different
opportunities sets, different choices.

00:33:53.430 --> 00:33:58.223
I think this is a fun way for you to
start playing around in that area and

00:33:58.223 --> 00:34:01.680
experimenting to find out how you decide.

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:06.336
So that's kind of an intended ripple
effect of this application process

00:34:06.336 --> 00:34:10.855
which is also why I love that,
our questions are open and meaningful.

00:34:10.855 --> 00:34:16.305
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great point, I mean,
leaders come in all shapes and sizes too.

00:34:16.305 --> 00:34:20.727
And we're not just admitting someone
with a specific leadership style.

00:34:20.727 --> 00:34:23.970
People have all kinds of
leadership styles and

00:34:23.970 --> 00:34:27.139
they can be valuable in
many different ways.

00:34:27.139 --> 00:34:31.578
And so, yeah,
this is one of those things where in

00:34:31.578 --> 00:34:35.614
the essay you have
the liberty to talk about.

00:34:35.614 --> 00:34:40.820
How you make decisions,
how much you value a particular thing and

00:34:40.820 --> 00:34:45.848
how that would impact the decisions
that you make in your life?

00:34:47.496 --> 00:34:48.850
&gt;&gt; Here's a question from Sega.

00:34:48.850 --> 00:34:54.489
So we've talked a lot about kind of the
essays, a story, how the pieces connect,

00:34:54.489 --> 00:34:58.439
how should I balance the detail
of resume versus impact.

00:34:58.439 --> 00:35:03.533
What types of information can I share
where that give you those levels and

00:35:03.533 --> 00:35:08.090
bits of evidence that really
clarify that picture?

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:14.080
But what happens if you're not sure
what that end goal is just yet?

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:18.013
So the question itself, how can applicants
who don't know exactly what path they

00:35:18.013 --> 00:35:19.728
want to take after business school.

00:35:19.728 --> 00:35:23.091
But rather want to use business school
as an opportunity to explore and

00:35:23.091 --> 00:35:25.833
really understand the different
pathways and options.

00:35:25.833 --> 00:35:30.898
How do you show that effectively in
an application without appearing confused?

00:35:30.898 --> 00:35:35.754
Any examples of how previous
applicants have done this successfully?

00:35:35.754 --> 00:35:39.293
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, that's a great question.

00:35:39.293 --> 00:35:43.524
I think,
I don't want people to feel like they

00:35:43.524 --> 00:35:47.550
have to have it all
figured out by any means.

00:35:47.550 --> 00:35:51.229
In fact, even though you have
the opportunity to share some of

00:35:51.229 --> 00:35:55.616
your aspirations and career goals,
and why Stanford would be a good fit,

00:35:55.616 --> 00:35:59.252
we don't hold true [LAUGH] to
what you write and why Stanford.

00:35:59.252 --> 00:36:04.293
It's an element that's important for
us to hear about

00:36:04.293 --> 00:36:10.440
what are the things that you're
ascribing to, but we certainly.

00:36:10.440 --> 00:36:14.669
And if you're part of
the Stanford ethos and community,

00:36:14.669 --> 00:36:20.571
it is all about learning and keeping
an open mind, and discovering new things,

00:36:20.571 --> 00:36:26.200
and having the support of the community
to help you think through that.

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:32.145
So to some extent, it should be a two year
exploration or journey for individuals.

00:36:32.145 --> 00:36:36.312
And it doesn't have to be, you might come
in and be like, I know I want to go from x

00:36:36.312 --> 00:36:39.920
to y and I'm pivoting or
I'm staying in the same career path.

00:36:39.920 --> 00:36:44.421
But I see the business education
as a way to get me there.

00:36:44.421 --> 00:36:50.280
That's great too, but I think there's
certainly some liberties here for

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:56.400
people to really use the two years
to think about what's next for them.

00:36:56.400 --> 00:37:00.661
We don't often get an opportunity
to take a step back and

00:37:00.661 --> 00:37:05.296
reflect, and that's what the MBA
program allows you to do.

00:37:05.296 --> 00:37:07.472
If your last step in your career and

00:37:07.472 --> 00:37:12.275
if been doing what you thought was
appropriate, this is the time in your life

00:37:12.275 --> 00:37:16.350
where you can really think about
what that next step looks like.

00:37:16.350 --> 00:37:20.546
So I'm looking at it just to make sure,

00:37:20.546 --> 00:37:26.230
how can you effectively
without looking confused?

00:37:26.230 --> 00:37:29.088
I think one thing that's important and

00:37:29.088 --> 00:37:33.592
is if you are sharing something
that's really, let's say,

00:37:33.592 --> 00:37:38.570
for example, you have a passion for
getting into media.

00:37:38.570 --> 00:37:43.650
And that is where you
want to see yourself go.

00:37:43.650 --> 00:37:48.904
If there's no evidence that you even
dabbled in that space prior to coming

00:37:48.904 --> 00:37:53.919
to business school, we may question,
why hasn't that come up.

00:37:53.919 --> 00:37:58.725
Whether it was tried to get involved
in some organization when you were

00:37:58.725 --> 00:38:00.933
an undergrad or even post grad.

00:38:00.933 --> 00:38:04.035
Or that perhaps you had an externship, and

00:38:04.035 --> 00:38:09.563
you enabled yourself to do it in a space
where you could kind of explore that.

00:38:09.563 --> 00:38:14.659
It is helpful to see some element
if it is a passion of yours that

00:38:14.659 --> 00:38:20.346
you want to pursue that there's
some evidence that you've explored

00:38:20.346 --> 00:38:25.610
a little bit about that before
getting to the business school.

00:38:25.610 --> 00:38:27.424
I don't know, that's my thought,

00:38:27.424 --> 00:38:30.772
I might just actually question if
I don't see enough that shows me.

00:38:30.772 --> 00:38:33.761
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sometimes that kind of
exploration might be in your

00:38:33.761 --> 00:38:34.617
essays to read.

00:38:34.617 --> 00:38:38.316
Because not everybody might have had
the opportunity to like dabble in

00:38:38.316 --> 00:38:39.930
the world of VC.

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:44.240
But what is it about venture capital
that might speak to you, right?

00:38:44.240 --> 00:38:46.234
So even if it's in your, what matters,

00:38:46.234 --> 00:38:50.300
or somewhere to let us know,
where does this interest come into play?

00:38:50.300 --> 00:38:51.920
How does this fit in?

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:56.783
So I actually think, Sega,
you might be under estimating that

00:38:56.783 --> 00:39:00.475
how much of a direction
you might already have.

00:39:00.475 --> 00:39:03.693
I think exploration in and
of itself is totally valid and

00:39:03.693 --> 00:39:07.127
a really relevant reason to
go to business school, right?

00:39:07.127 --> 00:39:11.777
Stephanie just talked about it's
baked into the programme pretty much,

00:39:11.777 --> 00:39:16.654
I think 70, 75% of our students do at
least one pivot in some way before.

00:39:16.654 --> 00:39:23.040
And, There must be something
you're interested in exploring.

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:26.275
Maybe it's a field, maybe it is a role,

00:39:26.275 --> 00:39:31.200
maybe it is an impact that
you're looking to make.

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:34.322
So even as you're thinking
about exploration and

00:39:34.322 --> 00:39:37.519
the different options,
there are going to be ways for

00:39:37.519 --> 00:39:42.190
you to narrow that down into something
that feels bite sized, right?

00:39:42.190 --> 00:39:45.555
The only people or the only person you
need to convince that this is a valid

00:39:45.555 --> 00:39:47.938
reason to go to business school is you,
it's you.

00:39:47.938 --> 00:39:50.100
&gt;&gt; It's you, yes-
&gt;&gt; And so a couple ways for

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:55.481
you to maybe think through,
all right, What could that be for

00:39:55.481 --> 00:39:59.450
me and even if it's an open-ended,
it's open-ended in that direction.

00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:03.547
This feels very plugging
one of our other events.

00:40:03.547 --> 00:40:05.833
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But in sharing your story one,

00:40:05.833 --> 00:40:10.291
starting your story,
I actually walk folks through a couple of

00:40:10.291 --> 00:40:14.765
different exercises to help get
those creative juices going.

00:40:14.765 --> 00:40:17.343
&gt;&gt; Yeah
&gt;&gt; One of the questions you might

00:40:17.343 --> 00:40:21.903
be able to ask yourself is,
if there was anything that you could do,

00:40:21.903 --> 00:40:26.704
no preconceived notions of what
success was like, no fear of failure,

00:40:26.704 --> 00:40:29.840
anything that you want to do,
what would it be?

00:40:30.920 --> 00:40:36.249
Do some brainstorming, do some free
writing, talk to people about that and

00:40:36.249 --> 00:40:41.330
you might be surprised at how much focus
you might already have or how much

00:40:41.330 --> 00:40:46.910
kind of dimension and interest in me you
can add to that exploration element.

00:40:46.910 --> 00:40:49.494
So, definitely ways to do it and

00:40:49.494 --> 00:40:53.816
you probably already know
more than you think you do.

00:40:53.816 --> 00:40:57.717
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and what I do love,
that's so well said, well,

00:40:57.717 --> 00:41:01.210
I hope that people take that to heart.

00:41:01.210 --> 00:41:06.277
What I do love about the community
at the GSB is that if you are doing

00:41:06.277 --> 00:41:11.253
that exploration, you're going
to be surrounded by people who

00:41:11.253 --> 00:41:15.970
instead of telling you why it
can't be done or questioning.

00:41:15.970 --> 00:41:20.853
They're really going to be open
up to tell me more about that and

00:41:20.853 --> 00:41:26.310
how can I help support you and
really allowing you to dig into that and

00:41:26.310 --> 00:41:31.027
find out if it's something
worth pursuing or not as well.

00:41:31.027 --> 00:41:35.606
You might have a number of
different hypotheses that you test

00:41:35.606 --> 00:41:37.413
during your two years.

00:41:37.413 --> 00:41:41.279
&gt;&gt; So
we have about seven minutes remaining.

00:41:41.279 --> 00:41:46.220
Stephanie, I think we can actually do
a lightning round of what's remaining.

00:41:46.220 --> 00:41:49.390
So let's do that and then we can close
with any final words of advice we have.

00:41:49.390 --> 00:41:52.159
So, would you view
candidates with 14 years,

00:41:52.159 --> 00:41:56.700
how do you view candidates with
14 years of work experience?

00:41:56.700 --> 00:42:00.651
&gt;&gt; With someone who has a lot
of experience worth sharing,

00:42:00.651 --> 00:42:05.946
we certainly value the perspective
that you could bring to the classroom,

00:42:05.946 --> 00:42:10.568
given the experience you've had,
and we see that as a value add in

00:42:10.568 --> 00:42:14.790
the classroom to be able
to bring that perspective.

00:42:14.790 --> 00:42:17.484
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
it's not about how long you've worked, but

00:42:17.484 --> 00:42:19.640
what have you done during that time?

00:42:19.640 --> 00:42:23.575
Number of years of work experience, age,
those are not parts of our evaluation

00:42:23.575 --> 00:42:27.320
process, it's more about what
footprints have you left behind?

00:42:27.320 --> 00:42:28.566
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:42:28.566 --> 00:42:32.707
&gt;&gt; All right, next one, any practical
suggestions on how to solve when seams

00:42:32.707 --> 00:42:37.390
sound banal when put together, even though
the individual incidences are unique.

00:42:37.390 --> 00:42:39.050
So how to tie things together?

00:42:39.050 --> 00:42:40.318
I'll take this one first, Stephanie.

00:42:40.318 --> 00:42:40.929
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:42:40.929 --> 00:42:45.165
&gt;&gt; So even if, plugging another event,
yesterday sharing your story too,

00:42:45.165 --> 00:42:48.854
writing in the application,
we did a what matters most to you and

00:42:48.854 --> 00:42:51.060
why kind of thought exercise.

00:42:51.060 --> 00:42:52.660
So we did a quick version there.

00:42:52.660 --> 00:42:57.351
There is also already posted a more
extended version of that workshop that I

00:42:57.351 --> 00:42:59.210
did earlier in the year.

00:42:59.210 --> 00:43:02.316
So definitely check out our
Stanford GSB YouTube channel,

00:43:02.316 --> 00:43:04.720
there are going to be
a bunch of videos there.

00:43:04.720 --> 00:43:08.800
We're really working on building out the
number of resources that you all can use

00:43:08.800 --> 00:43:10.840
to help guide you through this process.

00:43:10.840 --> 00:43:13.545
We want everybody to know that,
yes you can do this,

00:43:13.545 --> 00:43:17.930
here are tools to help you put
your best foot forward, all right.

00:43:17.930 --> 00:43:24.450
Let's see, understanding the parts of
the application, the process is holistic.

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:28.822
Are there certain parts that you feel
applicants tend to overlook in terms of

00:43:28.822 --> 00:43:31.931
importance to the broader
evaluation process?

00:43:31.931 --> 00:43:35.439
&gt;&gt; No I don't think there's
a general theme, I think no.

00:43:35.439 --> 00:43:37.850
&gt;&gt; Yeah, same, no general theme.

00:43:37.850 --> 00:43:40.610
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:43:40.610 --> 00:43:41.934
&gt;&gt; Here's a question,

00:43:41.934 --> 00:43:46.419
nowadays it seems that it has become
fashion to mention social impact and

00:43:46.419 --> 00:43:47.091
NGO work.

00:43:47.091 --> 00:43:50.420
That's the goal behind pursuing an MBA.

00:43:50.420 --> 00:43:54.143
How do we as the admissions committee
distinguish true motivation behind

00:43:54.143 --> 00:43:56.130
students applying to the MBA program?

00:43:57.690 --> 00:43:59.993
Okay, so that might not be so
much of a lightning round type question.

00:43:59.993 --> 00:44:02.003
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, right?

00:44:02.003 --> 00:44:07.484
[COUGH] Well, we hope that when
you are sharing whatever it

00:44:07.484 --> 00:44:13.535
is that's important to you that
it is part of the application,

00:44:13.535 --> 00:44:19.609
as I agree that everything in
here is as authentic as possible.

00:44:19.609 --> 00:44:24.881
And so no, you do not have to fit
a [LAUGH] of having any desire for social

00:44:24.881 --> 00:44:30.484
impact necessarily, but what is important
is that you are authentically sharing

00:44:30.484 --> 00:44:35.544
the values that are important to you
&gt;&gt; Yeah, if nonprofit, if NGO,

00:44:35.544 --> 00:44:40.650
if social impact is not what drives you,
you don't have to.

00:44:40.650 --> 00:44:44.060
I am not looking for
someone who wants to do that necessarily.

00:44:44.060 --> 00:44:47.823
There's another question in the Q&amp;A box,
do I have to want to change the world?

00:44:47.823 --> 00:44:52.500
No, that's not at all something that
we're looking at as like a necessity.

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:53.312
This is about,

00:44:53.312 --> 00:44:56.940
how do you want to almost even think
about changing your life, right?

00:44:56.940 --> 00:45:00.631
Going to business school is going to help
you look at the world through different

00:45:00.631 --> 00:45:02.294
lenses, so changes can be small.

00:45:02.294 --> 00:45:02.830
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:45:02.830 --> 00:45:07.359
&gt;&gt; And meaningful, but that's going to
depend on you and what your priorities and

00:45:07.359 --> 00:45:08.410
your values are.

00:45:08.410 --> 00:45:11.565
So don't listen to the chatter,
center yourself, and

00:45:11.565 --> 00:45:13.781
you're going to be the most effective and

00:45:13.781 --> 00:45:17.826
compelling when you're talking about
the things that you care about.

00:45:17.826 --> 00:45:18.665
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:45:18.665 --> 00:45:21.516
&gt;&gt; And here is one more question and

00:45:21.516 --> 00:45:25.960
maybe we can close on this and
then go into advice.

00:45:25.960 --> 00:45:28.321
How do we view failure?

00:45:28.321 --> 00:45:32.460
I think there's might be actually a couple
different questions here about failure,

00:45:32.460 --> 00:45:34.940
let me make sure I'm getting it properly.

00:45:34.940 --> 00:45:37.363
Is it something you find
it hard to understand,

00:45:37.363 --> 00:45:39.570
what experience would have been like?

00:45:39.570 --> 00:45:43.930
What do you say, how will we advise-
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and someone else said,

00:45:43.930 --> 00:45:46.770
can applicants talk about their failures-
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:45:46.770 --> 00:45:49.250
&gt;&gt; They have positively.

00:45:49.250 --> 00:45:52.920
So I think it's absolutely
you can talk about a failure.

00:45:52.920 --> 00:45:58.909
Entrepreneurs will say fail
often to succeed sooner,

00:45:58.909 --> 00:46:05.690
so there's value in what you
learn from the failure, right?

00:46:05.690 --> 00:46:10.045
So I think the failure itself
can feel like just that, but

00:46:10.045 --> 00:46:15.410
it's actually what your outcome
is as a result of the failure.

00:46:15.410 --> 00:46:19.895
Do you use that as something that
impedes you going forward that

00:46:19.895 --> 00:46:23.290
you have the fear of not
being able to overcome?

00:46:23.290 --> 00:46:28.092
Or has the failure itself
taught you to look at things

00:46:28.092 --> 00:46:32.800
in a different way and
overcome those challenges?

00:46:32.800 --> 00:46:36.792
So I think failure is
a natural part of life and

00:46:36.792 --> 00:46:42.151
actually is a framework and
fabric of who you are as a person,

00:46:42.151 --> 00:46:46.984
how you've grown and
that's very meaningful, and so

00:46:46.984 --> 00:46:52.640
take those as opportunities to
convey what you've learned.

00:46:52.640 --> 00:46:54.369
That's what I would say.

00:46:54.369 --> 00:46:58.570
&gt;&gt; All right, and Stephanie people have
heard from me enough throughout the week.

00:46:58.570 --> 00:47:03.130
Any final parting words, words of wisdom,
words of encouragement that you

00:47:03.130 --> 00:47:07.260
can share with everybody who
have shared time with us today?

00:47:07.260 --> 00:47:12.307
&gt;&gt; Sure, I mean,
[LAUGH] I think at a very high level,

00:47:12.307 --> 00:47:15.673
there's some anxiety and fear or

00:47:15.673 --> 00:47:20.480
concern about my gosh,
should I even apply,

00:47:20.480 --> 00:47:26.750
the acceptance rate is so
low I couldn't possibly get in.

00:47:26.750 --> 00:47:33.700
And I would just say that 100% sure far
way of not getting in is not to apply.

00:47:33.700 --> 00:47:38.311
So the most encouraging advice
I can give to everyone is

00:47:38.311 --> 00:47:40.926
just to put your foot forward.

00:47:40.926 --> 00:47:47.209
That that is going to give you the best
opportunity for us to learn about you.

00:47:47.209 --> 00:47:52.374
And so I encourage everyone on
the line to take that leap of faith

00:47:52.374 --> 00:47:57.154
to believe in yourself to put
your best foot for and apply.

00:47:57.154 --> 00:48:01.737
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for
sharing this time with us.

00:48:01.737 --> 00:48:05.125
Stephanie, thank you for
letting me pick your brains,

00:48:05.125 --> 00:48:08.224
chit chat about a profession and
job that we love and

00:48:08.224 --> 00:48:12.640
being able to support folks through
it is just the most amazing feeling.

00:48:12.640 --> 00:48:15.195
So, thank you,
thank you everybody out there.

00:48:15.195 --> 00:48:17.860
I hope to cross paths with you again soon.

00:48:17.860 --> 00:48:20.036
Until then, stay safe.

00:48:20.036 --> 00:48:21.360
